From f2b78a5d536cdfd65c25a6d4fbd2bb6ef25acf84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
In-Reply-To: <178113124323.90620.6403136846887207198@gmail.com>
References: <178113124323.90620.6403136846887207198@gmail.com>
From: Charles Pellegrini <c4ffein.work@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 18:59:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH v3 4/5] lib: scatterlist: reject zero-size splits with -EINVAL

sg_calculate_split() has no defined result for a zero entry in
split_sizes[], and each position such a zero can take misbehaves
differently:

 - With input entries remaining past it, the zero-size split is
   assigned one zero-length output entry. The DMA API provides no
   defined behavior for zero-length segments, DMA engines have needed
   explicit guards against them (78efb76ab4df ("dmaengine: rcar-dmac:
   Reject zero-length slave DMA requests")), and the block layer
   disallows them outright (9b2e0016d04c ("bvec/iter: disallow
   zero-length segment bvecs")).

 - Landing exactly when the input is exhausted, the split keeps
   nents == 0, its output array allocation returns ZERO_SIZE_PTR, and
   the out_sg[-1] fixup in sg_split_phys()/sg_split_mapped() is an
   out-of-bounds write (a wild write landing outside any valid
   mapping -- oopses under KUnit; also reproduced as a heap overflow
   with a userspace ASAN harness).

 - In that same position with further non-zero sizes after it, the
   !size loop-exit check in sg_calculate_split() additionally mistakes
   the request for satisfied: sg_split() returns success with the
   trailing splits silently unfulfilled.

There is no good empty value to hand back either. The CPU view of a
scatterlist has no empty representation (the end-of-list marker lives
on the last entry). The DMA view has a documented terminator --
consumers of a mapped list may stop on the first sg_dma_len() == 0
entry (include/linux/scatterlist.h) -- but carrying it requires
allocating an entry whose CPU view is then a zero-length element, the
shape rejected above, and sg_split_mapped() already leaves
dma_len == 0 on the trailing entries of ordinary shorter mapped views,
so overloading it as "empty split" would be ambiguous. A zero landing
at input exhaustion has no allocated entry to carry a sentinel at all.

Reject any zero split size upfront with -EINVAL. With every accepted
size >= 1, each split on the success path consumes at least one input
byte, so nents >= 1 always holds and the out_sg[-1] fixups stay in
bounds.

No in-tree caller passes a zero size. The two callers whose sizes
could arithmetically reach zero already branch around the call
(dthev2-aes.c's cryptlen == 0 guard, spi-omap2-mcspi.c's
transfer_reduction test), and the nearest in-tree consumer of split
outputs rejects empty lists with the same errno
(sec_alloc_and_fill_hw_sgl() in drivers/crypto/hisilicon/sec/).

The out-of-bounds write and the test-argument issue that exposed this
family were flagged by an automated review of the v1 posting; the
silent-success case by its review of v2.

Fixes: f8bcbe62acd0 ("lib: scatterlist: add sg splitting function")
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260601175549.b4a10c07dd9e3b867f66da0c@linux-foundation.org/
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5 hegel-c
Signed-off-by: Charles Pellegrini <c4ffein.work@gmail.com>
---
 lib/sg_split.c | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/sg_split.c b/lib/sg_split.c
index ab574dd26b03..058ccc9b446e 100644
--- a/lib/sg_split.c
+++ b/lib/sg_split.c
@@ -129,7 +129,8 @@ static void sg_split_mapped(struct sg_splitter *splitters, const int nb_splits)
  * @in_mapped_nents: the result of a dma_map_sg(in, ...), or 0 if not mapped.
  * @skip: the number of bytes to skip in the input sg list
  * @nb_splits: the number of desired sg outputs
- * @split_sizes: the respective size of each output sg list in bytes
+ * @split_sizes: the respective size of each output sg list in bytes; every
+ *               size must be non-zero
  * @out: an array where to store the allocated output sg lists
  * @out_mapped_nents: the resulting sg lists mapped number of sg entries. Might
  *                    be NULL if sglist not already mapped (in_mapped_nents = 0)
@@ -144,7 +145,8 @@ static void sg_split_mapped(struct sg_splitter *splitters, const int nb_splits)
  * etc ...
  * It will be the caller's duty to kfree() out array members.
  *
- * Returns 0 upon success, or error code
+ * Returns 0 upon success, or error code; a zero entry in @split_sizes is
+ * rejected with -EINVAL.
  */
 int sg_split(struct scatterlist *in, const int in_mapped_nents,
 	     const off_t skip, const int nb_splits,
@@ -155,6 +157,10 @@ int sg_split(struct scatterlist *in, const int in_mapped_nents,
 	int i, ret;
 	struct sg_splitter *splitters;
 
+	for (i = 0; i < nb_splits; i++)
+		if (!split_sizes[i])
+			return -EINVAL;
+
 	splitters = kzalloc_objs(*splitters, nb_splits, gfp_mask);
 	if (!splitters)
 		return -ENOMEM;
